{
 "thread": "YibwYpjrOaQ",
 "subject": "sessions within sessions",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2019-11-18T17:29:00Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "Uul-mebBBgAJ",
   "from": "David Bernasconi",
   "author": "c6847fe2e1ee86d3",
   "date": "2019-11-18T14:27:44Z",
   "subject": "sessions within sessions",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello,<div><br></div><div>I have a 24 session study design but i would like to create estimates based on groups of sessions if possible since there are some similarities between them. Is there a a way that I can get estimates based on groups of sessions?  IE the first 6 sessions would be in group one, the next 6  in group 2 , etc...</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div><br></div><div>David</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "_AdMlw1tAwAJ",
   "from": "Chris Sutherland",
   "author": "58a62321f08f30f8",
   "date": "2019-11-18T14:37:18Z",
   "subject": "RE: [oscr] sessions within sessions",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\">Hi David, <u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\">Yes, this is definitely possible and just requires adding that information as covariates, either for traps or for the state space.<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\">Example 1 – density (24 session, groups of 6 share an attribute) <u></u><u></u></span></u></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">for(i in 1:24){<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">  ssDF[[i]]$group &lt;- factor(rep(1:4,each=6)[i],levels=1:4)<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">}<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\">Now you can use group directly in the density model<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\">Example 2: detection <u></u><u></u></span></u></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\">In this case you would add the group covariate to the tdf directly before constructing the scrFrame.<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\">Now you can use group directly in the detection model<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\">Example 2: sigma<u></u><u></u></span></u></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">#make the scrFrame then:<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">scrFrame$sigCovs$group &lt;- factor(rep(1:4,each=6),levels=1:4)<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\">Now you can use group directly in the sigma model<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\">Hope this helps<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\">Chris<u></u><u></u></span></p></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:#000066\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\">From:</span></b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">oscr_p...@googlegroups.com</a> &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">oscr_p...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt; <b>On Behalf Of </b>David Bernasconi<br><b>Sent:</b> Monday, November 18, 2019 9:28 AM<br><b>To:</b> oSCR &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">oscr_p...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt;<br><b>Subject:</b> [oscr] sessions within sessions<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Hello,<u></u><u></u></p><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">I have a 24 session study design but i would like to create estimates based on groups of sessions if possible since there are some similarities between them. Is there a a way that I can get estimates based on groups of sessions?  IE the first 6 sessions would be in group one, the next 6  in group 2 , etc...<u></u><u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Best,<u></u><u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">David<u></u><u></u></p></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
